"OP-00851: Bounded weakening for constrained invertibility — clopen.solutions (https://clopen.solutions/OP-00851/)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; # Bounded weakening for constrained invertibility ... Source: Bjerkevik (2026), Conjecture 6.10 (arXiv 2023); the case c = 3 is a slight weakening of a conjecture in the appendix of his PhD thesis (2020).\n\nOP-00851 Open\n\n# Bounded weakening for constrained invertibility\n\nSubmit a result\n\nFix a field $k$. A constrained invertibility problem of size $n$ is a pair $(P, Q)$ of $n \\times n$ matrices with entries in $\\{0, *\\}$. It is feasible over $k$ if there are $A, B \\in k^{n \\times n}$ with $AB = I_n$ such that $A_{ij} = 0$ whenever $P_{ij} = 0$ and $B_{ij} = 0$ whenever $Q_{ij} = 0$; a star allows any entry, including zero. Let $G(P,Q)$ be the directed bipartite graph on $u_1, \\ldots, u_n, v_1, \\ldots, v_n$ with an edge $u_i \\to v_j$ when $P_{ji} = *$ and an edge $v_j \\to u_i$ when $Q_{ij} = *$, and let $d_G(x,y)$ be the length of a shortest directed path from $x$ to $y$ ($+\\infty$ if there is none).\n\nConstrained invertibility problems were introduced by Bjerkevik and Botnan, and used by them, and with Kerber, to prove that the interleaving distance between multiparameter persistence modules is NP-hard to compute and to approximate within a factor less than $3$. Bjerkevik and Botnan gave a feasible problem of size three that admits no solution in which $A$ is a permutation matrix, so $c = 1$ does not suffice below. Bjerkevik proved that, for each odd $c$, the condition below holds for every feasible problem if and only if any two $\\epsilon$-interleaved pointwise finite-dimensional upset decomposable persistence modules admit a $c\\epsilon$-matching.\n\nProblem. Is there an odd integer $c \\ge 1$, independent of $n$, $P$ and $Q$, such that every feasible problem admits a permutation $\\sigma$ of $\\{1, \\ldots, n\\}$ with $$d_G(u_i, v_{\\sigma(i)}) \\le c \\quad\\text{and}\\quad d_G(v_{\\sigma(i)}, u_i) \\le c \\qquad (1 \\le i \\le n)?$$ Equivalently, after adding every edge between the two parts that joins the ends of a directed path of length at most $c$ in $G(P,Q)$, the resulting problem is feasible with $A$ a permutation matrix and $B = A^{-1}$. The field is fixed; no uniformity in $k$ is required.\n\nSource: Bjerkevik (2026), Conjecture 6.10 (arXiv 2023); the case c = 3 is a slight weakening of a conjecture in the appendix of his PhD thesis (2020).\n\n## Classification\n\nPrimary\n    CO — Combinatorics\nSecondary\n    AT, RA\nKeywords\n    AIM Open Applied Problems, constrained invertibility problems, interleaving distance, multiparameter persistence\n\n## References\n    \n    @article{bjerkevik2026stabilizing,\n      author        = {Bjerkevik, H{\\aa}vard Bakke},\n      title         = {Stabilizing decomposition of multiparameter persistence modules},\n      journal       = {Found. Comput. Math.},\n      volume        = {26},\n      number        = {2},\n      year          = {2026},\n      pages         = {939--998},\n      doi           = {10.1007/s10208-025-09695-w},\n      eprint        = {2305.15550},\n      archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n    }\n    @phdthesis{bjerkevik2020thesis,\n      author = {Bjerkevik, H{\\aa}vard Bakke},\n      title  = {Stability and Complexity in Multi-Parameter Persistence},\n      school = {Norwegian University of Science and Technology (NTNU)},\n      year   = {2020},\n      url    = {https://hdl.handle.net/11250/2656497},\n    }\n    @inproceedings{bjerkevik2018complexity,\n      author    = {Bjerkevik, H{\\aa}vard Bakke and Botnan, Magnus Bakke},\n      title     = {Computational complexity of the interleaving distance},\n      booktitle = {34th International Symposium on Computational Geometry ({SoCG} 2018)},\n      series    = {LIPIcs},\n      volume    = {99},\n      publisher = {Schloss Dagstuhl--Leibniz-Zentrum f{\\\"u}r Informatik},\n      year      = {2018},\n      pages     = {13:1--13:15},\